## The finding

`crimes_00022` — **Sync I/O in Hot Path**, `src/api/files.ts:12–15`, confidence 0.90. Evidence, verbatim:

```
"`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)",
"`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)",
"`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)",
"lines: 12, 13, 15",
"swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it"
```

Summary line: `"3 synchronous I/O calls inside domain function — each invocation blocks the event loop for the duration of the I/O. Async counterparts exist for every method captured."`

## The blocking calls

Three, all from `node:fs`, all inside `loadPlanSchema()`:

| Line | Call | Blocks on |
|---|---|---|
| 12 | `existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | `stat(2)` |
| 13 | `statSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | `stat(2)` again |
| 15 | `readFileSync(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8")` | `open` + `read` + `close` |

That's **three blocking filesystem round-trips per call**, plus a synchronous `JSON.parse` of the whole file. Every one of them parks the single event-loop thread — no other request's callback, timer, or socket read can make progress while they run. Under concurrency that serializes: with N in-flight requests, request N waits for N−1 full disk round-trips before its handler even starts. That is exactly the shape of "used to be milliseconds, now p99 spikes and lag is reported" — the per-call cost didn't change, the queueing behind it did.

This is also why caching would be the wrong fix here: it hides the cost on the hit path while leaving a cold start, a cache miss, or an invalidation able to reintroduce the stall. The event-loop block is the defect.

## Smallest async migration

The signature stays `loadPlanSchema()` — same name, same zero parameters, same `null` / parsed-value return contract. Only the return type gains its `Promise` wrapper, which is unavoidable for any async migration:

```ts
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";

export async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {
  let raw: string;
  try {
    raw = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8");
  } catch (err) {
    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "ENOENT") return null;
    throw err;
  }
  if (raw.length === 0) return null;
  return JSON.parse(raw);
}
```

Two things collapse at once, and both are strictly smaller than a mechanical 1:1 swap:

- **Three I/O round-trips become one.** `existsSync` was only guarding against a missing file, and `statSync` only against an empty one — `readFile` already tells you both (`ENOENT`, and an empty string). Swapping each call for its async twin would preserve three awaits for no reason.
- **The TOCTOU race goes away.** `existsSync` → `statSync` → `readFileSync` re-resolves the path three times; a delete between line 12 and line 15 threw an unhandled `ENOENT`. One `readFile` inside `try` closes that window as a side effect.

Behaviour is preserved on both `null` paths: `stat.size === 0` and `raw.length === 0` are true for exactly the same file (an empty file yields an empty string under `utf8`). A malformed-JSON file still throws from `JSON.parse`, as before.

**Call sites:** `loadPlanSchema` is exported and has no in-repo callers (`grep -rn 'loadPlanSchema' .` returns only the definition at `src/api/files.ts:11`), so the change is contained to this file. It is part of the package's public surface, though — external callers now need `await loadPlanSchema()`, and TypeScript will flag every one of them, so the migration is compiler-checked rather than silent. If the package is published, this is a breaking change worth a major bump; if the handler that calls it lives outside this fixture, that handler needs to be `async` too.

`adminUrl()` at line 18 is untouched — no I/O.

Two unrelated findings sit in the same file and are *not* the latency cause, so I've left them alone: `crime_00007` (`/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9, a hardcoded home path) and `crime_00006` (`localhost:3000` @L8). Both are real portability bugs worth their own fix, but neither blocks the event loop.
